{"subjectId":"python","id":"sorting","title":"Sorting & Custom Ordering","group":"Data Structures","order":20,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">Python's sort is fast (Timsort), <strong>stable</strong>, and customised with a <code>key</code> function — rarely with a comparator. Master <code>key=</code> and most ordering problems become one line.</p>\n<section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-arrows-sort\"></i><code>sorted()</code> vs <code>list.sort()</code></h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>nums = [5, 2, 9, 1]\nprint(sorted(nums), nums)          # [1, 2, 5, 9] [5, 2, 9, 1]\nresult = nums.sort(reverse=True)   # in place\nprint(result, nums)                # None [9, 5, 2, 1]\nprint(sorted(\"hello\"))             # ['e', 'h', 'l', 'l', 'o']\nprint(sorted({\"b\": 1, \"a\": 2}))    # ['a', 'b']   ← iterating a dict gives keys</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th></th><th><code>sorted(iterable)</code></th><th><code>lst.sort()</code></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Works on</td><td>Any iterable (str, dict, set, generator…)</td><td>Lists only</td></tr><tr><td>Returns</td><td>A <strong>new</strong> list</td><td><code>None</code> — sorts in place</td></tr><tr><td>Memory</td><td>Copies the data</td><td>No extra list</td></tr><tr><td>Options</td><td><code>key=</code>, <code>reverse=</code></td><td><code>key=</code>, <code>reverse=</code></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"callout callout--warn\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Watch out</div><p><code>xs = xs.sort()</code> sets <code>xs</code> to <code>None</code>. Either <code>xs.sort()</code> or <code>xs = sorted(xs)</code>.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-key\"></i>The <code>key</code> function</h3>\n<p><code>key</code> is called <strong>once per item</strong>; items are ordered by those computed keys. Any callable works: a built-in, a method, a lambda, an <code>operator</code> helper.</p>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>words = [\"banana\", \"Apple\", \"fig\", \"cherry\"]\nprint(sorted(words))                           # ['Apple', 'banana', 'cherry', 'fig']\nprint(sorted(words, key=str.lower))            # ['Apple', 'banana', 'cherry', 'fig']\nprint(sorted(words, key=len))                  # ['fig', 'Apple', 'banana', 'cherry']\nprint(sorted(words, key=lambda w: w[-1]))      # ['banana', 'Apple', 'fig', 'cherry']\nprint(sorted([-4, 1, -2, 3], key=abs))         # [1, -2, 3, -4]</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--note\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Key point</div><p>Uppercase letters sort before lowercase (<code>'Z' &lt; 'a'</code>) because strings compare by code point. Use <code>key=str.lower</code> (or <code>str.casefold</code> for full Unicode) for human order.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-list-numbers\"></i>Multi-level ordering</h3>\n<p>Return a <strong>tuple</strong> from the key: ties on the first element fall through to the second. Negate numbers to flip one level to descending.</p>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>students = [(\"asha\", 90, 21), (\"ravi\", 85, 22), (\"kiran\", 90, 20), (\"meera\", 85, 20)]\n\n# marks descending, then name ascending\nprint([s[0] for s in sorted(students, key=lambda s: (-s[1], s[0]))])\n# ['asha', 'kiran', 'meera', 'ravi']</code></pre></figure>\n<figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>['asha', 'kiran', 'meera', 'ravi']</code></pre></figure>\n<p>Can't negate a string? Use <strong>stability</strong>: sort by the secondary key first, then by the primary key. Each pass keeps the previous order among ties.</p>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>people = [(\"asha\", \"dev\"), (\"ravi\", \"ops\"), (\"kiran\", \"dev\"), (\"meera\", \"ops\")]\nby_name_desc = sorted(people, key=lambda p: p[0], reverse=True)   # secondary first\nresult = sorted(by_name_desc, key=lambda p: p[1])                 # then primary\nprint(result)   # [('kiran', 'dev'), ('asha', 'dev'), ('ravi', 'ops'), ('meera', 'ops')]</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--def\"><p><strong>Stable sort</strong> — items with equal keys keep their original relative order. Python's sort is guaranteed stable, which makes multi-pass sorting correct.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-settings\"></i><code>operator.itemgetter</code> &amp; <code>attrgetter</code></h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>from operator import itemgetter, attrgetter\nfrom dataclasses import dataclass\n\nrows = [{\"name\": \"ravi\", \"age\": 30}, {\"name\": \"asha\", \"age\": 25}]\nprint([r[\"name\"] for r in sorted(rows, key=itemgetter(\"age\"))])   # ['asha', 'ravi']\n\npairs = [(2, \"b\"), (1, \"z\"), (2, \"a\")]\nprint(sorted(pairs, key=itemgetter(0, 1)))    # [(1, 'z'), (2, 'a'), (2, 'b')]\n\n@dataclass\nclass Emp:\n    name: str\n    salary: int\n\nstaff = [Emp(\"a\", 50), Emp(\"b\", 70), Emp(\"c\", 60)]\nprint([e.name for e in sorted(staff, key=attrgetter(\"salary\"), reverse=True)])   # ['b', 'c', 'a']</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-book-2\"></i>Sorting dict items</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>scores = {\"asha\": 91, \"ravi\": 78, \"kiran\": 91, \"zoe\": 85}\n\ntop = sorted(scores.items(), key=lambda kv: (-kv[1], kv[0]))\nprint(top[:3])                        # [('asha', 91), ('kiran', 91), ('zoe', 85)]\nprint(dict(sorted(scores.items())))   # {'asha': 91, 'kiran': 91, 'ravi': 78, 'zoe': 85}\nprint(max(scores, key=scores.get))    # asha   ← first max wins</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-arrows-exchange\"></i>Comparators with <code>cmp_to_key</code></h3>\n<p>When the order can't be expressed as a key — it depends on <em>both</em> items together — write a comparator returning negative / zero / positive and wrap it with <code>functools.cmp_to_key</code>.</p>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>from functools import cmp_to_key\n\ndef largest_number(nums):\n    \"\"\"Arrange numbers to form the largest possible number.\"\"\"\n    strs = [str(n) for n in nums]\n    def cmp(a, b):\n        if a + b &gt; b + a:\n            return -1          # a goes first\n        if a + b &lt; b + a:\n            return 1\n        return 0\n    strs.sort(key=cmp_to_key(cmp))\n    result = \"\".join(strs)\n    return \"0\" if result[0] == \"0\" else result\n\nprint(largest_number([3, 30, 34, 5, 9]))   # 9534330\nprint(largest_number([0, 0]))              # 0</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-abc\"></i>Natural sort &amp; custom objects</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>import re\n\nfiles = [\"file10.txt\", \"file2.txt\", \"file1.txt\"]\nprint(sorted(files))     # ['file1.txt', 'file10.txt', 'file2.txt']\n\ndef natural_key(s):\n    return [int(t) if t.isdigit() else t.lower() for t in re.split(r\"(\\d+)\", s)]\n\nprint(sorted(files, key=natural_key))   # ['file1.txt', 'file2.txt', 'file10.txt']\n\nclass Task:\n    def __init__(self, pri, name):\n        self.pri, self.name = pri, name\n    def __lt__(self, other):             # sort() only needs &lt;\n        return self.pri &lt; other.pri\n    def __repr__(self):\n        return self.name\n\nprint(sorted([Task(3, \"c\"), Task(1, \"a\"), Task(2, \"b\")]))   # [a, b, c]</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-binary\"></i>min, max, heapq &amp; bisect take a key too</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>import heapq, bisect\n\nwords = [\"kiwi\", \"banana\", \"fig\", \"apple\"]\nprint(min(words, key=len), max(words, key=len))       # fig banana\nprint(heapq.nsmallest(2, words, key=len))             # ['fig', 'kiwi']\nprint(max([], default=\"empty\"))                       # empty\n\nrecords = [(1, \"a\"), (4, \"b\"), (9, \"c\")]              # sorted by first field\nprint(bisect.bisect_left(records, 5, key=lambda r: r[0]))   # 2   ← key= needs 3.10+</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Need</th><th>Best tool</th><th>Cost</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Full order</td><td><code>sorted</code> / <code>.sort</code></td><td>O(n log n)</td></tr><tr><td>Just the min or max</td><td><code>min</code> / <code>max</code></td><td>O(n)</td></tr><tr><td>Top k (k small)</td><td><code>heapq.nsmallest</code> / <code>nlargest</code></td><td>O(n log k)</td></tr><tr><td>Keep a list sorted while inserting</td><td><code>bisect.insort</code></td><td>O(log n) search + O(n) insert</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-info-circle\"></i>Timsort facts &amp; pitfalls</h3>\n<div class=\"grid\"><div class=\"card\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-bolt\"></i>Timsort</h3><p>Hybrid merge + insertion sort. O(n log n) worst case, <strong>O(n)</strong> on already-sorted or reversed data — it detects natural runs.</p></div><div class=\"card\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-anchor\"></i>Stable</h3><p>Guaranteed. Equal keys keep their input order.</p></div><div class=\"card\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-alert-triangle\"></i>Mixed types</h3><p><code>sorted([1, 'a'])</code> raises <code>TypeError</code> — Python 3 won't compare unrelated types.</p></div><div class=\"card\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-clock\"></i>Key once</h3><p>Keys are computed once per item (decorate-sort-undecorate), so an expensive key is still only n calls.</p></div></div>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>try:\n    sorted([3, \"a\", 1])\nexcept TypeError as e:\n    print(e)   # '&lt;' not supported between instances of 'str' and 'int'\nprint(sorted([3, None, 1], key=lambda x: (x is None, x or 0)))   # [1, 3, None]</code></pre></figure>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>How do you sort by one field descending and another ascending?</summary><div><p>Use a tuple key and negate the numeric field: <code>key=lambda r: (-r.score, r.name)</code>. If the descending field isn't numeric, rely on stability: sort by the secondary key first, then by the primary key with <code>reverse=True</code>.</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>Is Python's sort stable, and why does it matter?</summary><div><p>Yes — Timsort is stable. Equal elements keep their relative order, which lets you sort in multiple passes (secondary key first) and get a correct multi-key ordering.</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>When would you use cmp_to_key?</summary><div><p>When ordering depends on comparing two items together and can't be turned into a per-item key — e.g. \"largest number\" (compare <code>a+b</code> with <code>b+a</code>). Otherwise a key function is simpler and faster.</p></div></details></section>"}